A vector database is a database specially designed to store:

Embedding Vector+Original Text+Metadata

For every chunk, it stores something like:

Chunk:
"FastAPI supports Dependency Injection."

Vector:
[0.23, 0.76, -0.45, ...]

Metadata:
{
    file_name: "FastAPI.pdf",
    page: 42,
    author: "Arun",
    topic: "Dependency Injection"
}

So unlike FAISS, it doesn't store only vectors.

It stores everything related to the chunk.

Popular Vector Databases
1. FAISS

Type:

✅ Library

Pros:

Very fast
Local
Free
Easy to learn

Cons:

Limited metadata support
No server
No authentication
Not ideal for large production systems

Use for:

Learning
Desktop apps
Local RAG
2. ChromaDB ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Type:

✅ Open-source Vector Database

Pros:

Easy to use
Stores vectors
Stores metadata
Stores documents
Persistent storage
Perfect for beginners

Example:

collection.add(

documents=[],

embeddings=[],

metadatas=[],

ids=[]
)

This is why most LangChain tutorials start with ChromaDB.

3. Pinecone

Type:

✅ Cloud Vector Database

Pros:

Fully managed
Scales to millions of vectors
Authentication
High availability
Production ready

Cons:

Paid after free tier

Companies use it because they don't have to manage servers.

4. Qdrant

Very popular today.

Features:

Metadata filtering
REST API
Docker support
Cloud
Fast

Many startups use Qdrant.

5. Milvus

Designed for:

Billions of vectors
Large AI applications
Distributed systems

Common in enterprise environments.

6. Weaviate

Adds:

Semantic search
Hybrid search
Graph-like relationships
APIs

Often used in enterprise knowledge systems.

Why We'll Learn ChromaDB

Because it is the best bridge between learning and production.

It teaches:

Collections
Documents
Embeddings
Metadata
Searching
Filtering

Later, if you switch to Pinecone or Qdrant, 90% of the concepts remain the same.

Only the API changes.
